{
  "id": "number-of-provinces-union-find",
  "questionId": "number-of-provinces-union-find",
  "slug": "number-of-provinces-union-find",
  "title": "Number of Provinces",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Union find",
  "patterns": [
    "union find",
    "connected components"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "a province is just a connected component - how do you count connected components incrementally as you scan the matrix?",
    "initialize each city as its own parent in a union-find structure",
    "for every pair (i, j) with isConnected[i][j] = 1, union i and j",
    "use path compression and union by rank/size so each union/find call is nearly O(1)",
    "the answer is the number of distinct roots left at the end"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "re-scanning the full matrix with DFS/BFS when union-find can do it in one pass over the upper triangle",
    "forgetting path compression, causing find() to degrade toward O(n) per call on skewed unions",
    "counting isConnected[i][i] as a real connection and double-processing the diagonal"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given n cities, and a direct-connection matrix `isConnected` where `isConnected[i][j] = 1` means city i and city j are directly connected by a road, and `isConnected[i][j] = 0` means they are not. A province is a maximal group of cities that are connected to each other, directly or indirectly through other cities.\n\n**Return the total number of provinces.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 200",
      "isConnected is an n x n matrix",
      "isConnected[i][i] = 1 for all i",
      "isConnected is symmetric"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single city (1x1 matrix)",
      "every city is its own province (identity matrix)",
      "all cities form one giant province",
      "connections that require chaining through an intermediate city to see the full province"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "isConnected = [[1,1,0],[1,1,0],[0,0,1]]",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "cities 0 and 1 are connected, city 2 is on its own"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "isConnected = [[1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1]]",
        "output": "3",
